{
  "id": "chem-005",
  "title": "What does pH tell us about a liquid?",
  "category": "chemistry",
  "subject_area": "acids and bases",
  "difficulty_level": "introductory",
  "target_audience": [
    "general public",
    "primary school",
    "secondary school"
  ],
  "central_question": "What makes a liquid an acid or a base, and what do the numbers on the pH scale mean?",
  "template": "principle",
  "principle_statement": "pH is a number from 0 to 14 that tells how acidic or basic a water-based liquid is: below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral and above 7 is basic.",
  "domain_of_validity": "Liquids with water in them; strong acids and bases can be dangerous, so this topic is about the idea, not a home experiment.",
  "scientific_explanation": {
    "level_1": "Some liquids are acids, some are bases, and some are neither. pH is a number that tells you which. The scale runs from 0 to 14. Pure water sits in the middle at 7, which is neutral. A number below 7 means the liquid is acidic, like lemon juice. A number above 7 means it is basic, like soapy detergent. The further from 7, the stronger it is.",
    "level_2": "pH measures how acidic or basic a liquid that has water in it is. Water that has more free hydrogen ions is acidic, and water that has more free hydroxyl ions is basic. Pure water is neutral, with a pH of 7.0. Vinegar and lemon juice are acidic, while laundry detergents and ammonia are basic. Each whole step on the scale is ten times stronger than the next, so a pH of 4 is ten times more acidic than a pH of 5.",
    "level_3": "The pH scale is logarithmic. Because each whole number is a factor of ten, a mine-drainage water five pH units below neutral is 100,000 times more acidic than neutral water. The concentration of hydrogen ions, c = n / V, rises as the pH falls. Rainfall is generally a little acidic, with a pH a bit below 6."
  },
  "supporting_evidence": [
    "Pure water has a pH of 7, which is neutral.",
    "Vinegar and lemon juice are acidic, while laundry detergents and ammonia are basic."
  ],
